{"judgments": [
 {"clause_id": "c051", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This section permits Tenant to carry the property coverage, including full replacement value coverage on the buildings, \"through Tenant's self-insurance\" with no cap, while Section 12.1 caps self-insurance for liability at $500,000. A court could be asked whether the building is required to be insured with a carrier at all.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c052", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The indemnity reaches any accident \"arising directly or indirectly out of the business conducted in the Premises or occurring in, on, or about the Premises,\" carving out only Landlord's gross negligence or willful misconduct, so it appears to cover Landlord's own ordinary negligence. The qualifier \"unless Tenant is compensated therefor by insurance\" gives no test for when Tenant is so compensated.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.26}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c053", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c054", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Landlord indemnifies only for its \"gross negligence or willful misconduct\" and is excused where the damage results from Tenant's negligence, with no rule for concurrent fault. Whether Landlord owes anything where both parties are negligent is left unstated.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c055", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c056", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c057", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Landlord's approval right is confined to components \"which adversely affect[] the structural integrity of the Building,\" a phrase with no stated threshold or method of determination. The following sentence's exception — \"except as provided for in this Lease\" — does not identify which other provisions restrict Improvements.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c058", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The alternative obligation \"to otherwise secure the Landlord to its satisfaction promptly upon request\" gives Landlord an unbounded discretion that sits against Section 25.12's rule that consents and approvals shall not be unreasonably withheld. What security satisfies Landlord is unmeasurable on the face of the section.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.13}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c059", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The unqualified duty to \"promptly repair and restore\" after any casualty must be read against Section 15.4, which lets Tenant terminate and hand the proceeds to Landlord in a late-term casualty, and against Section 15.2's escrow mechanism. Whether restoration is owed pending the Section 15.4 election is unstated.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c060", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Payment into \"an escrow mutually satisfactory to Landlord and Tenant\" is an agreement to agree with no fallback escrow agent or terms if the parties disagree. The section is also silent on how it operates where a fee mortgagee claims the proceeds under Section 18.2.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c061", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The absolute bar on surrender, termination and abatement after damage cannot be squared with Section 15.4, which expressly permits Tenant to terminate for a late-term casualty, or with Section 16.2's rent abatement during restoration. A court could be asked which provision governs.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c062", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Termination trigger (a) — that \"the time necessary to repair or rebuild the Building exceeds sixty (60) days\" — is satisfied by almost any material casualty, so the two conditions do not sit as apparent equals. The direction to give notice \"to the other\" is also a mechanical error, since only Tenant is given the termination right.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c063", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c064", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The section makes Rent \"equitably adjusted\" on termination and \"equitably apportioned according to the floor space so taken\" otherwise, without a formula or decision-maker for what is equitable. It also directs both that Rent \"shall abate during the period of restoration\" and that it be apportioned by floor space, without saying how the two interact.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c065", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The award is given wholly to Landlord \"whether such damages shall be awarded as compensation for diminution in value to the leasehold,\" yet the proviso preserves to Tenant \"any other damages allowed Tenant by law,\" a catch-all that can be read to swallow the grant. Which clause captures a leasehold-value award is contestable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.26}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c066", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Default clause (b) — failure to \"diligently proceed, after Tenant is aware of same (including by telephonic notice ... to the store manager)\" to cure structural maintenance failures — carries no written notice and no fixed cure period, unlike clauses (a) and (c). What counts as awareness and as diligent proceeding is left undefined.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.26}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c067", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Tenant's remedy of abating Base, Percentage and Additional Rent until a judgment is satisfied cannot easily be squared with Section 4's requirement that Rent be paid \"without deduction or setoff for any reason whatsoever\" and Section 7's waiver of any diminution of Rent. A court would have to decide whether Section 7's \"explicitly reserved\" carve-out saves this remedy.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26},
   {"issue": "The enumeration is broken: the section runs into a clause \"(b)\" concerning quiet possession with no preceding \"(a),\" and then uses \"(a)\" and \"(b)\" again for remedies. Which notice period — thirty days or five days — attaches to which default is obscured by the defective drafting.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c068", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The declaration that all remedies are cumulative and non-exclusive sits against Section 18.3's exculpation limiting Tenant to Landlord's interest in the Premises and Section 7's waiver of specified remedies. Whether cumulation restores remedies those sections take away is contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c069", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c070", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The right to enter and \"perform any act required of Tenant under any provision of this Lease\" is not squared with Section 25.4, which limits Landlord's entry to inspection, showings and Landlord's own repair obligations on twenty-four hours' notice. Whether Landlord may enter to cure is left in doubt.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c071", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Prime Rate\" is defined as \"the base rate of interest ... established in the Wall Street Journal,\" but the Journal publishes a prime rate survey rather than establishing a \"base rate.\" Which published figure the parties meant is a question a court could be asked.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c072", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Interest at Prime plus two percent on every late amount overlaps the five percent late charge that Section 4.4 fixes as \"liquidated damages\" for the same lateness. Whether Landlord may collect both on the same overdue installment is unaddressed.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c073", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The certifying party must supply \"[a]ny other information reasonably requested,\" an open-ended catch-all after six enumerated specifics, and must certify that all Landlord conditions \"have been satisfied (except as stated).\" The scope of what may be demanded within fifteen days is not bounded.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c074", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Subordination here is \"upon the express condition\" of nondisturbance, while Section 1 of the Exhibit E form the Tenant must sign subordinates the Lease \"at all times ... in all respects\" without that condition. Section 25.3 makes the Lease prevail over exhibits, so which controls a mortgagee's demand is contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c075", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The promise that \"Landlord shall remain fully liable on this Lease\" for pre-transfer matters cannot be squared with the following paragraph of the same section, which frees the named Landlord \"entirely ... of all obligations of Landlord hereunder\" upon sale. A court could be asked which survives a transfer.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c076", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The declaration that there is \"no personal (or entity) liability of Landlord\" and that Tenant may look solely to Landlord's interest in the Premises conflicts with the preceding paragraph of Section 18.3 keeping Landlord \"fully liable\" for pre-transfer defaults, and with Section 17.2's contemplation of a \"final monetary judgment against Landlord.\" Which limits Tenant's recovery is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c077", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Tenant's \"absolute right ... without Landlord's further approval\" is immediately qualified by requiring Landlord's cooperating documents to be \"in a form acceptable to Landlord and Landlord's counsel\" and by conditions Landlord \"may\" impose. Whether Landlord can effectively block a leasehold mortgage through form objections is the resulting ambiguity.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c078", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c079", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The flat warranty that \"Landlord is the fee owner of the Land\" is difficult to square with Section 18.3, which defines Landlord to include an owner of \"a leasehold interest in the Land (if Landlord's interest is that of a lessee under a ground lease),\" and with Section 18.2's subordination to \"any ground lessor.\" Whether a ground lease may exist is left unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c080", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The representation that there are no mortgages or security interests except as set forth in Exhibit G, which lists only two easements, sits against Sections 18.2, 26.1, 6.2.2 and 12.1, all of which assume an existing mortgagee of the fee. A court could be asked whether the representation was breached or merely surplus boilerplate.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c081", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The use representation is doubly hedged — \"To the knowledge of Landlord\" and only \"after securing all necessary governmental permits and approvals\" — so it may warrant nothing about present zoning or permit availability. Its content sits awkwardly against Section 24.1's unqualified statement that the Premises comply with all existing law.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c082", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c083", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c084", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c085", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c086", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c087", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The covenant of quiet enjoyment is made \"subject to the performance by Tenant of the terms and conditions of this Lease,\" which can be read to forfeit it on any breach however trivial and without regard to the cure periods in Section 17.1. Its interaction with Section 17.2's five-day quiet-possession cure right is not stated.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c088", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Tenant must surrender \"Improvements made by Tenant (but excluding Tenant's personal property and trade fixtures),\" and the Lease nowhere draws the line between Tenant's Work deemed Improvements under Section 5.2 and its removable trade fixtures. The string of casualty and eminent domain exceptions is also drafted so that \"casualty damage\" appears twice on different conditions.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c089", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The definition sweeps in all requirements \"otherwise relating to the environment or solid, liquid or hazardous substances,\" a general catch-all beyond the enumerated federal statutes, with no limit as to future laws. Its outer boundary is left to construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.10}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c090", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Hazardous Material\" extends to anything \"regulated as 'hazardous,' 'toxic' or under any other similar designation,\" an open-ended catch-all, and expressly includes petroleum products and by extension the powder and fuels Tenant sells. Its breadth against Section 23.3's acknowledgment of Tenant's firearms and fuel inventory invites construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c091", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c092", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c093", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The first sentence is an unqualified representation that Landlord provided \"full, accurate and complete copies\" of all environmental reports, while every following warranty in the same section is limited to knowledge \"without any independent inquiry or investigation.\" Which standard applies to the disclosure obligation is contestable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c094", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c095", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Tenant covenants against any storage of Hazardous Material \"except in strict accordance with all Environmental Laws,\" while the following acknowledgment says the firearms, powder and fuel operation \"does not violate any of the provisions of this Section\" on the same condition, so the acknowledgment adds no protection. Whether ordinary retail stock of powder and petroleum products breaches the covenant is left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c096", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The exclusion for material \"placed inside the four walls of the Building or on, in or under the Premises by an invitee or customer of Tenant\" is broad enough to strip Landlord's indemnity of most practical content, since almost any on-site material is inside the Premises. Whether the invitee/customer limitation modifies both branches of the exclusion is grammatically unclear.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c097", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Tenant's indemnity covers \"the introduction onto the Premises of any Hazardous Material by a Tenant Indemnified Person\" without limiting the introduction to the Lease term, while Landlord's mirror exclusion in Section 23.4 is expressly limited to \"during the term of this Lease.\" The temporal reach of Tenant's obligation is left unstated.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c098", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Tenant must remediate \"promptly ... and after Landlord approval,\" a condition with no time limit or standard for Landlord's approval, which cannot easily be squared with the duty to commence promptly under Environmental Laws. The notice duty is also carved out for \"any Hazardous Material which Tenant sells in the ordinary course of business, such as black powder,\" a category with no boundary.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c099", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The unqualified statement that the Premises comply with all existing law as of the Commencement Date sits against Section 5.1, by which Tenant accepts the Building \"as is, with all faults and defects ... without representation or warranty.\" A court could be asked whether the compliance representation survives the as-is acceptance.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
