   {"issue": "The general rule that consents shall not be unreasonably withheld is qualified only by \"[e]xcept as expressly provided otherwise,\" and several sections give a party \"sole and absolute discretion\" (Sections 5.4, 8.1, 26) while others, like Section 5.5's \"location acceptable to Landlord and Tenant,\" state no standard at all. Which consents this section reaches is contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c113", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Landlord's waiver of any lien on Tenant's inventory and equipment sits against Section 22, which lets Landlord \"remove and dispose of\" Tenant's trade fixtures and personal property at Tenant's expense on surrender. Whether the waiver survives a holdover or default is unstated.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.13}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c114", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The representation is that there are no other brokerage claims, but the indemnity extends only to \"any such claim related to a broker hired by such party,\" a narrower category that leaves claims by unhired brokers unallocated. The gap between the warranty and the indemnity is on the face of the section.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.13}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c115", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c116", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Holdover Base Rent at \"two hundred percent (200%)\" is fixed without regard to fault and may be attacked as a penalty, and the section does not say whether Percentage Rent and the Section 4.5 credit continue \"upon the same terms and conditions.\" What accrues during a holdover month is therefore uncertain.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c117", "issues": [
   {"issue": "A blanket \"time shall be of the essence\" cannot easily be squared with the cure periods and diligence standards in Sections 17.1 and 17.2 and with the extensions granted by Section 25.9's force majeure clause. Whether any missed deadline is a material breach is the resulting question.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c118", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The offer must be accepted \"by April 16, 2004,\" the very date as of which the Lease is stated to be entered into, so the acceptance window is a single day or already closed. The consequence is also optional and one-sided — the offer \"may, at Tenant's sole option, be terminated\" — leaving the Lease's effectiveness on late acceptance uncertain.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c119", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c120", "issues": [
   {"issue": "A termination right exercisable in Tenant's \"sole and absolute discretion\" after the Contingency Date — which under Section 26.1 can fall on or after the Commencement Date — is hard to square with Section 7's waiver of any right to cancel and Section 5.1's as-is delivery. Whether Tenant may still walk after taking possession is contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c121", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The condition that \"any party holding a mortgage or other similar interest in the Land has executed a Subordination Agreement\" presupposes a mortgagee, while Section 19.3 represents that no mortgages or security interests affect the Land except the Exhibit G easements. Whether the condition is satisfied vacuously or requires an instrument is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c122", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c123", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c124", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The condition requires that \"all blanks, bracketed references and incomplete exhibits\" be completed, yet the executed instrument leaves Exhibit B a bracketed list of items \"[t]o Show\" and Exhibit C's dates blank. Whether the failure of this condition gives Tenant a termination right, or the exhibits are simply of no effect, is contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c125", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This section conditions Tenant's obligations on a parking ratio of at least 4.2 spaces per 1,000 square feet, while Section 16.2 treats a fall below 3.75 spaces per 1,000 square feet as the trigger for termination after a taking. The Lease thus sets two different adequate-parking standards without reconciling them.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c126", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"[M]aterial adverse change in the marketability of the Premises\" gives no measure of materiality or of marketability, and the list is garbled by repetition (\"the environmental condition of the Premises or the Premises, the condition of the Building and the Premises\"). What change permits termination cannot be determined from the words.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c127", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Landlord must \"use reasonable efforts and diligence to enforce all of the provisions\" of easement and operating agreements, a standard the Lease nowhere defines or benchmarks. The prohibition also reaches \"any agreements with respect to the Premises,\" a general phrase whose breadth against the enumerated easement, operation and governmental agreements is contestable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c128", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The waiver reaches \"any matters whatsoever arising out of or in any way connected with this Lease, Tenant's use or occupancy of the Premises, and/or any claim of injury or damage,\" the last limb untethered to the Lease at all. Whether it covers a personal injury or tort claim between the parties unconnected with the Lease is a question of scope.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c129", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The metes-and-bounds description contains a broken call — \"to a point at its intersection with the division on the west and the property now or formerly owned by said Louis Tokos and Irene Tokos\" — omitting the line intended, and the exceptions carve out unspecified \"any other property dedicated to the State of New York or the City of Johnson City.\" The boundary of the Land, and hence the Premises, is therefore not closed by the text.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.18}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c130", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Commencement, Delivery and Rent Commencement Dates are left blank in the executed form, so the confirmation Section 2.3 requires supplies none of the dates on which the term and rent turn. Whether the Lease's dates were ever fixed depends on evidence outside the instrument.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c131", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The form fixes expiration in \"2019,\" fifteen calendar years from 2004, while Section 2.2 expires the Lease at the end of the fifteenth Lease Year as defined in Section 2.4 and Section 3.3 automatically extends to the following January 31. The printed year and the Lease's own mechanism can produce different expiration dates.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c132", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The form describes the options as automatically exercised unless Tenant \"presents notice\" by stated dates, which are left blank, whereas Section 3.2 fixes the deadline as one year before expiration of the term then in effect. Which statement of the deadline controls is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c133", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The recital states that \"Landlord owns the parcels\" constituting the Restricted Property, while the Declaration is made and the restrictions are undertaken by the \"Restricted Party,\" an affiliate rather than Landlord. Whose land is actually burdened is left in doubt by the recital.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c134", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The recital describes the Restricted Party simply as \"an affiliate of Landlord,\" while Section 9.1 of the Lease defines Restricted Parties to include entities controlled by or affiliated with another entity controlled by or affiliated with Landlord. Whether the Declaration reaches the full class Section 9.1 covers is contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.13}
 ]}
]}

