input.content:
{"judgments": [
 {"clause_id": "c135", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The restriction is conditioned on the Premises being \"used for the Specific Excluded Use (as defined herein),\" but the definition is supplied only at the end of the same sentence and describes uses of the Restricted Property, making the trigger circular. The five percent limb — \"to the extent that such sales, in the aggregate, exceed five percent (5%) of its retail sales\" — leaves \"its\" ungrounded and fixes no measuring period.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38},
   {"issue": "The list of restricted uses here omits \"sale of other casual apparel,\" which Section 9.1 of the Lease includes in its otherwise identical definition of Specific Excluded Use. Whether the recorded Declaration burdens the same activities the Lease covenant does is therefore contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c136", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Termination \"on the expiration or termination of the Gander Mountain Lease\" sits against Section 1's own condition that the restrictions run only while the Premises are used for the Specific Excluded Use, giving two different end points. Which event releases the Restricted Property first is not stated.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c137", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The acknowledgment that \"damages are not adequate\" cannot be squared with Section 9.2 of the Lease, which gives Tenant a right to damages as well as injunctive relief for breach of the same restrictions. A court could be asked whether Tenant may recover damages for a Declaration breach.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c138", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c139", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The recital that \"Landlord owns the property\" is stated flatly, while Section 18.3 of the Lease contemplates that Landlord's interest may be that of a lessee under a ground lease and Section 18.2 contemplates a ground lessor. What estate the Mortgage encumbers is left open by the recital.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.11}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c140", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The recital equates the whole \"Property\" with the leased \"Premises,\" while Section 1 of the Memorandum of Lease describes the Premises as lying on \"the portion of the property\" described in its Exhibit A. Whether the nondisturbance protection covers the entire tract or only part of it turns on this.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c141", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c142", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The recital states flatly that \"Tenant has agreed to subordinate the Lease,\" whereas Section 18.2 of the Lease makes any subordination conditional on recognition of the Lease and nondisturbance. A party could argue the recital states an unconditional agreement the operative Lease does not give.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c143", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Subordination \"at all times ... in all respects\" without qualification cannot be squared with Section 18.2 of the Lease, which makes subordination expressly conditional on the Lease's validity being recognized and possession undisturbed. Section 25.3, making the Lease prevail over exhibits, sharpens the question of which governs.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.25}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c144", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Lender's promise that it \"will be bound by all of the terms of the Lease\" is difficult to square with Section 3(d) of the same agreement, which frees Lender from liability for Landlord defaults, from prior offsets, and from amendments made without its consent. The nondisturbance protection is also made conditional on the opaque proviso \"but only provided the terms of this Section are complied with.\"", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c145", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c146", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c147", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c148", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause ends with a dangling \"and (d)\" that belongs to the next subsection, so the sentence does not close on its own terms. It also obliges Tenant to take a new lease \"containing the same terms and conditions as contained in the Lease\" without saying who fixes the new commencement date or the effect of Tenant's refusal.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c149", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Subsection (ii) frees Lender from \"claims, defenses or offsets ... which arose or existed prior to the time Lender obtains possession,\" while proviso (B) preserves \"any rights of self-help, set-off, abatement or termination expressly provided in the Lease,\" which is precisely what Section 17.2 provides. The two limbs point in opposite directions on a pre-foreclosure Landlord default.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c150", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Lender cure right operates \"[n]otwithstanding any provisions of the Lease to the contrary,\" overriding the notice and cure scheme of Section 17.2 of the Lease, while Section 25.3 of the Lease says the Lease prevails over any exhibit. Which instrument governs a Tenant termination notice is contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c151", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Tenant's direction to pay all rent to Lender on written notice sits against Section 4 of the Lease, which requires Rent to be paid at Landlord's notice address \"or at such other place designated in writing by Landlord.\" Whose direction controls in the event of competing notices is addressed only by this exhibit.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.12}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c152", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Lender's obligation to make proceeds available is made \"subject to such reasonable and customary procedures ... as Lender may impose,\" while Section 15.2 of the Lease requires the proceeds to be held in an escrow \"mutually satisfactory to Landlord and Tenant.\" Which disbursement regime governs a restoration is left unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c153", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Lender's notice address is left entirely blank in the form, so the notices this agreement requires — including the Section 4 default notice on which Tenant's right to cancel depends — have no stated destination. Notices are also deemed given on the postmark date rather than on receipt.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c154", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The statement that this Agreement \"shall not modify or in any way affect Landlord's obligations under the Lease\" is hard to reconcile with Section 4, which conditions Tenant's cancellation rights notwithstanding the Lease, and Section 5, which redirects rent away from Landlord. Whether the Agreement alters the Lease bargain is precisely what is in doubt.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c155", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c156", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c157", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c158", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Tenant's \"absolute right ... without Landlord's or Lender's further approval\" is qualified in the next sentence by requiring Lender's documents to be \"in a form acceptable to Lender,\" with no reasonableness standard. Whether Lender can frustrate a leasehold financing through form objections is left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c159", "issues": [
   {"issue": "An entire-agreement clause superseding \"all prior or contemporaneous written ... agreements ... respecting the subject matter\" could be read to displace Section 18.2 of the Lease, which sets the conditions on which Tenant agreed to subordinate. Section 25.3 of the Lease says the Lease prevails over exhibits, creating the opposite result.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c160", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The description repeats the broken call \"to a point at its intersection with the division on the west and the property now or formerly owned by said Louis Tokos and Irene Tokos,\" omitting the line intended, and excepts unspecified property \"dedicated to the State of New York or the City of Johnson City.\" The Property subject to nondisturbance is therefore not closed by the text.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.13}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c161", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The recorded Memorandum describes the Premises as lying on \"the portion of the property legally described on Exhibit A,\" without identifying which portion, while the Lease demises the whole Building and Land as the Premises. What the recorded notice puts third parties on notice of is uncertain.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c162", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Memorandum has the initial term expiring \"on the fifteenth (15th) anniversary date of the Commencement Date,\" while Section 2.2 of the Lease expires it on the last day of the fifteenth Lease Year as defined in Section 2.4 and Section 3.3 extends it to the next January 31. The recorded expiration date and the Lease's mechanism can differ by months.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c163", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Memorandum records only that the Premises may be used \"for any lawful purpose, subject to certain specific prohibited uses,\" omitting the exclusivity and Landlord restrictions in Sections 8.1 and 9.1 that Tenant would want of record. What a purchaser is charged with notice of is narrower than the Lease grant.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.11}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c164", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Memorandum says it is \"executed in accordance with the terms of this Lease\" and that \"[t]he Lease is deemed to be a material part hereof,\" using \"this Lease\" in an instrument that is not the Lease. The mislabeling clouds whether the Memorandum incorporates the Lease of record or merely refers to it.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.12}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c165", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This copy of the legal description carries the same defective call — \"a point at its intersection with the division on the west and the property now or formerly owned by said Louis Tokos and Irene Tokos\" — leaving a boundary line unspecified in the recorded instrument. The open-ended exception for property \"dedicated to the State of New York or the City of Johnson City\" adds further indeterminacy.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.11}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c166", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Exhibit G is used twice for different purposes: Section 19.3 treats it as the schedule of Permitted Encumbrances, while Section 25.11 requires a Memorandum of Lease \"in the form attached as Exhibit G,\" which is the Exhibit F form. Which document Exhibit G supplies is left in conflict.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16},
   {"issue": "The first permitted encumbrance names \"Greenwood Builders Supply, Inc.,\" while the legal description of the Land repeatedly identifies the adjoining owner as \"Glenwood Builders Supply, Inc.\" A court could be asked whether the easement excepted is the one of record.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.12}
 ]}
]}

